WEBVTT

00:00:34.089 --> 00:00:47.264
And I welcome you to today's event and to today's topic, which is symbols and the third. This is basically the same thing, but the third sounds a little bit mysterious.

00:00:48.244 --> 00:01:01.318
And this goes back to a very fundamental concept and thought of Jung that he really saw a lot of opposites. Everywhere.

00:01:01.639 --> 00:01:25.729
So for him, it's a little bit like in electricity where you have a positive and a negative pole and the tension between them generates motion and light and everything. So by the movement from one pole to another, we get basically animating spirit. We get life.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:49.745
We got everything. And his idea is that these opposites are really acting in an antagonistic manner. So it's similar to this symbol of yin and yang, where in the Tao, where you have the dry snake and the wet snake, you have bright and the dark.

00:01:50.338 --> 00:02:09.875
And those are in competition and contrast to each other. So they are very different, and in the differentness, there has to be conflict. There has to be tension between them. And he really thinks that this is how human beings perceive and see the world.

00:02:10.805 --> 00:02:33.376
So everything is in context to the rest in an oppositional matter. So you can't really talk about high when you don't have a low. You can talk about bright when you don't have a dark. So talking about one thing implies always the existence of the other. In the context of yin, then it would be yang, and the other way around also.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:45.978
And this can be pretty well expressed and is seen very, very often, this oppositional manner and storytelling.

00:02:46.629 --> 00:03:10.329
So I found a lot of examples about this where it's often called juxtaposition or irony or just contrast, where you see always that you have two strong forces opposing each other. So it's always pretty abstract when you say it like this and they say, oh, good, good against evil.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:32.682
But As soon as you have opposites and they are together, things happen. And very often the story happens. So in the movie Up, for example, the animated Pixar movie, you have the old man who does not want to have everybody around because his wife died. And then you have the child who really wants to make friends.

00:03:32.955 --> 00:03:56.297
And the movie is basically about their relationship and how they change through their interaction. On Ferris Bueller's Day Off, you have Ferris, who is pretty the popular kid and just pretends to be sick to have a day off and takes his friend Cameron, who is pretty neurotic and lame, to be honest. And they spent the day.

00:03:57.891 --> 00:04:11.642
Breaking Bad, where you have Walter White and Jesse Pinkman, and one is a teacher, a former teacher and one is a former student, and they cook meth together, but are character-wise very, very different.

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:36.057
So you have opposites acting against each other, generating something, generating conflict, and in this way also development. But the problem is when you have these opposite acting against each other and being so different. It's hard to get them together.

00:04:37.802 --> 00:04:58.587
And this is a problem that Jung contended with in all his thoughts, because for him, the world consists of opposites, but we are not really able to perceive the opposites. Normally, we're just focusing on one part. And this is what consciousness can really do well for Jung.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:07.198
It can focus on one part of reality and focus only, for example, on the bright, forgetting about the dark. The other way around also.

00:05:08.256 --> 00:05:27.110
So the further consciousness pushes into one direction and tears apart one opposite in one direction, the other opposite starts acting up and becomes active and tries then to antagonize to reach back into equilibrium.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:48.203
So, and even though they're opposites, they're very, very close together. Once you push one part further and further, you will at a certain point run into the opposite. That's called enantiodromia.

00:05:49.072 --> 00:06:12.799
I think it's a Greek word and Jung was very interested in the origin of words because Nietzsche was also interested in the origin of words. And in the English language where you have this progression of good, better, best, the origin of best is bad.

00:06:15.006 --> 00:06:24.302
So at a certain point, when you reach the extreme, you get into the opposite. And that happens all by itself.

00:06:26.954 --> 00:06:51.204
And when you have, for example, the very strong opposite of male and female, and you want to combine those again, like all the opposites, and they can't really meet on the same field because they are two polar opposites of each other. There has to be an intermediate, and this intermediate is, uh, for Jung, the third.

00:06:52.114 --> 00:07:15.457
So when you combine male and female, you get out a child. And in that context, the child would be the third because the child has the parts of the male and the female, half, half. So it's a bridging between two opposites that could not reunite through other means, but rather through an intermediate who helps.

00:07:15.538 --> 00:07:31.796
A little bit like a translator. You have two people who only speak their own language and can't communicate with each other. There needs to be some kind of intermediate that's able to mediate and communicate between them, that speaks the language of both.

00:07:32.614 --> 00:07:54.528
And this you have also very often in stories again, where the hero, for example, has qualities which are often very good, but also very bad. For example, in Harry Potter, Harry Potter can speak language of snakes. Normally only the bad wizards can do that.

00:07:56.468 --> 00:08:21.119
So the hero is a mixture of both, of good and bad. Luke Skywalker in Star Wars. He's a Jedi Knight, but he's also the son of Darth Vader, who is the baddest person in the universe. So he has this part also in him. And this combination through a singular person is very often the protagonist and the hero combining both worlds.

00:08:22.365 --> 00:08:42.586
Another example, uh, would be Moses, who was a child of common people. I think of Jewish people, but then he grew up in this household of the pharaohs. So, or King Arthur, who grew up poor, but later found out that he has this secret heritage.

00:08:43.346 --> 00:09:02.816
So it is really this combination of two different worlds within the hero's journey, who is then uniting them, or rather changing them and the world through his actions because of her actions when, when it's a woman, by having lived in both worlds and bringing them together as a third, as a mediator.

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:25.459
And for Jung, the ultimate opposite was consciousness and unconscious, because consciousness is everything that you know that you can do, everything that you can relate to each other. Everything is connected in a kind of network. Everything fits very nicely. Everything is structured orderly.

00:09:26.085 --> 00:09:47.197
This is why consciousness, light is very often associated because in light you can see everything. Everything is clear. Everything is visible. But unconscious is characterized by everything which is not conscious. So it can't be ordered. It can't be brought to connection with the things you know. Everything is strange. Everything is intermingled.

00:09:47.261 --> 00:10:11.365
There's No clear separation where one thing ends and the other begins. And he says, this is a great dance of psychology that you have your consciousness and consciousness is supposed to grow and develop like a human being develops. When you are a child, you're very small. You can't do anything.

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:32.268
You can't talk, you can't walk. You're very dependent on other people. Basically all you can do is scream. But when you grow up, you get socialized, you learn things, you're able to play more and more complex games with yourself, with others, with the world. And it's this growing of consciousness trying to integrate more and more.

00:10:33.006 --> 00:10:49.913
So, but how can you really integrate things or come into contact with something which is completely strange and foreign, where you not even completely sure how to touch it when it comes out of the unconscious. And for Jung, that's the symbol.

00:10:50.411 --> 00:11:13.865
The symbol is the bridge and the mediator that is connecting both worlds of the consciousness and of the unconscious. And symbols for Jung are very often images. And these images, he says, are happening and are generated completely naturally.

00:11:15.530 --> 00:11:34.241
For example, in dreams, very often automatically. So for him, what is happening is you have all these conscious contents. So the stuff that you know and the things that you are aware of. He calls these associations when they're inside consciousness.

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:55.734
And the unconscious will take those parts and order them and structure them in a way which is weird and create a symbol. So symbol is something always that's coming out of the unconscious. But because it's in the middle, it's a third, it has parts of consciousness in form of these associations.

00:11:56.899 --> 00:12:14.785
And parts of the unconscious, which is more the pattern, the structure. So suddenly a symbol can be very paradoxical or very strange, like a mermaid, for example, which is a combination of a fish and a human being.

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:38.959
And you see that a lot in mythology where you have Medusa or the Hydra or the gods that are mixed together wildly. And the difference between a symbol and a sign, for example, is that a sign reduces complexity of the world and takes out individuality.

00:12:41.361 --> 00:12:54.116
So when you have a road sign and the road sign says dangerous road, you don't know really why it's dangerous. You're just being told it's dangerous and be careful. But there could be a lot of reasons why it's dangerous, but all the dangerous roads in the world, they get one sign.

00:12:54.871 --> 00:13:12.404
Or you say the sign tells you, okay, don't drive faster than 30, but there could be a lot of reasons why you're not allowed to drive faster than 30. It could be school nearby, the road could be bad, or road could be narrow, but that's not important for a sign. The sign is just reducing the complexity of the world.

00:13:13.496 --> 00:13:30.868
But the symbol, what a symbol does is pointing to something very complex, something unknown. It's just like the dragon's tail. You see part of it, but there's way more behind it. You see a little bit like a first iteration of something.

00:13:33.697 --> 00:13:58.171
So a symbol gets generated right at these edge of consciousness, the things that you know and you can do. And it is strange, mysterious, and grasping because you know, and your unconscious reacting to it, there's something to it. This has value for me. This is important. But you don't know really why.

00:13:58.332 --> 00:14:17.263
It's a little bit like picking up a new hobby or when you read something really interesting and then linger, or you listen to a song that is new and then it starts lingering around in the back of your head or somebody says something, there's your unconsciousness telling you, oh, there's something to unpack, there's something to explore.

00:14:19.078 --> 00:14:40.488
And this is the task and the duty of consciousness to unpack things. And symbols are structuring in that way that they get the attention and consciousness is supposed to enact with it and unpack it further and develop it. So symbol is not completely fixed forever.

00:14:41.452 --> 00:15:02.210
It can change and modulate based on the situation, based also on what state consciousness has. It's like the closest analogy you can have to something. It's like when somebody tries to explain to you, like I do right now, something which is new and weird.

00:15:03.190 --> 00:15:26.227
There are a lot of examples and a lot of analogies, saying, okay, it's something like that. It's something like that. Something like that. And this is the exact same thing that the unconscious does. It shows you things and say, yeah, look at this. It's a little bit like that. It's this combination. Jung has this example for this analogy making.

00:15:27.505 --> 00:15:42.140
When the Spanish people came to North America for the first time and brought horses with them, That was the first time there were horses in North America. So the people had no idea what a horse is because they never saw it because they're not local to North America.

00:15:42.799 --> 00:16:01.411
So how the Native Americans would describe it, they would say it's a tall pig because they had pigs and a horse is a little bit like a pig because it has this long nose, it has these pointy ears, and it has a little bit of a tail. So it is some similarity there.

00:16:01.813 --> 00:16:16.822
And bringing in these similarities is supposed to educate consciousness and bringing it to investigate more into what is really happening there.

00:16:18.145 --> 00:16:32.294
And these symbols can have an individual meaning for the person, of course, but they can also have a collective meaning. For example, in religion, you have a lot of symbology.

00:16:33.176 --> 00:16:48.357
In Christianity, you have this symbol of the cross, which is a very condensed part about so much of different stories. It's about Jesus. It's about how you're supposed to live your life.

00:16:49.586 --> 00:17:04.260
It's about the fundamentals of Christianity, and it's all condensed in a symbol that can grab the attention of the people and cause them to put it everywhere.

00:17:07.046 --> 00:17:29.947
There are also symbols like the swastika of the National Socialists, which urges people just to put it everywhere. And it expresses a psychological situation. So it's the unconscious communicating with consciousness, but it does not necessarily mean that consciousness understands what's happening.

00:17:30.043 --> 00:17:45.874
Because the interesting thing about the swastika of the Nazis is it's a very old symbol that's thousands of years old, but not in the way how the Nazis drew it. So it can move in two different directions. It can move to the left or to the right.

00:17:46.419 --> 00:18:07.605
And the interesting thing is, when it's moving to the right, which is what you find in India, it's always a very affirming symbol for consciousness. So it's moving toward consciousness when it's moving right. But the Nazi symbol, it was moving to the left. And this movement symbolizes movement into the unconscious.

00:18:08.374 --> 00:18:30.597
So into a less aware, less differentiated, less sophisticated state. So it's very interesting that they chose that symbol to put it everywhere because it's a return to barbarism and rather primitive, unsophisticated psychology, what the National Socialists did.

00:18:32.087 --> 00:18:55.173
But this is what human beings are experiencing everywhere in their personal life, but also outside, is these symbols coming up and they're really hitting the point of the time. Like the fish for the Christian age, symbolizing something about the condition of people, how it was back then, their psychological needs.

00:18:58.498 --> 00:19:19.553
And of course, these symbols can age. Like the cross has not the same meaning, or let's say feeling, the experiencing of the cross is not the same as 2,000, 1,000, or 500 years ago. This is why these symbols, they can age and they can die, but they can also be reborn again in a cultural context.

00:19:20.982 --> 00:19:45.276
And the important thing that Jung really stresses about this is that you cannot really take symbols literally. As they are, can be very confusing and very often paradoxes because it's basically showing the limits of consciousness. It's showing where consciousness is not enough because that's the normal state. Consciousness is not enough.

00:19:45.517 --> 00:20:07.888
You don't know enough. You don't do enough. You can't perceive enough. All these things. So consciousness is supposed to expand and it's trying to show, okay, these are your limitations, these are the things you have to deal with. And this makes him very different, for example, from Freud in the context of dream interpretation.

00:20:09.821 --> 00:20:31.435
For Freud, all the things, all the images that come up during dreaming are a facade. So it's always something is hidden. So you don't really care about what you see because it's a facade, a lie anyways. It has no meaning except for being there to distract and to lie to the dreamer.

00:20:34.420 --> 00:20:52.058
But Jung saw Dreams and the images that are generated in Dreams as the best approximation of something. It's like this horses-look-like-pigs mechanism of saying, okay, yeah, you don't know that yet, but it is like this and it will make sense afterwards.

00:20:54.336 --> 00:21:17.095
Where he says you're dreaming of something, you're experiencing something, but that's not supposed to be taken literally, but symbolically. That means it does not stand for itself. There's more behind it. You have to follow the dragon's tail to the rest of the dragon to understand what it's really about. Because a symbol or image can be very disagreeable.

00:21:17.789 --> 00:21:37.838
So you can think that you might kill someone or that you eat something. That is, for example, rotten. So this is not to be taken literally and say, oh, now I have to eat rotten fruit, but rather this seems to be that needs to be integrated in you that looks disagreeable.

00:21:40.410 --> 00:21:55.436
So it is this compensatory function of symbols and of the unconscious, which is constantly broadcasting things to consciousness to steer it in certain directions and to bring development and to bring growth.

00:21:57.826 --> 00:22:19.665
And I have a big nice list of examples of symbols that I wanted to talk about since the last time where I talked about dreams. Because on the one side Jung says there is no real handbook for it.

00:22:19.890 --> 00:22:42.231
So he says he doesn't think highly of dream books. It goes back to this idea of you can't take things literally. You have to take them symbolically, and you also have to always have context, the psychology of the dreamer, their situation, how their consciousness is set up, what is their personality.

00:22:42.311 --> 00:22:53.187
And so also Dream Interpretation is rather tedious and long with a lot of background knowledge also in mythology, religion, storytelling, and so on.

00:22:55.345 --> 00:23:20.020
But, but on this flip side, Jung is very direct regarding certain symbols and certain images where he says, ah yeah, this means always that. So On the one hand, he's saying there is no book, but on the other hand, he acts like symbols have a very fixed meaning if they are of a specific type.

00:23:22.302 --> 00:23:35.221
So the most standard is the symbol of water. Any body of water, it could be the sea, it could be the ocean. This is associated for him with the unconscious.

00:23:36.469 --> 00:24:00.618
As things can disappear in the water or they can suddenly come up, like fishes, for example, symbolizing thoughts as they can suddenly appear out of nothing, like a thought or an idea you can have. Or fire being associated with libido, the animating spirit, same as bodies are warm, which is normally the sign that something is alive.

00:24:01.854 --> 00:24:18.222
Especially for mammals, that it has a body heat that's radiated. And when the animal dies, then this heat is gone. So to have this association between, ah, you have movement, you have life, and this is associated with heat.

00:24:19.089 --> 00:24:42.889
And light being associated with consciousness, as you can see conscious things, and the dark being associated with the unconscious. And he is also pretty open and says, okay, when you dream of animals, those are symbolizing the lower forms of the— not the lower forms, that's incorrect.

00:24:43.403 --> 00:25:04.963
The lower levels of psychology, as he says, that the psychology of human beings is structured in a way that things are built on top of each other. And certain things are just at the bottom where we are similar to, for example, other mammals that exist or reptiles or even insects or plants.

00:25:07.630 --> 00:25:22.225
And yeah, that's, that's a good notion. The Indian swastika is also some sign of the sun. So the sun wheel is very old, very ancient, and people have been drawing suns since forever.

00:25:27.648 --> 00:25:39.229
So you have animals and animals symbolizing not only instincts, but also certain spheres of your consciousness and even parts of your nervous system.

00:25:39.846 --> 00:25:53.280
For example, reptiles symbolizing parts of your sympathetic nervous system and Of course, you can also dream or experience certain plants.

00:25:54.632 --> 00:26:13.307
And his idea about especially the tree was the unfolding of the personality over time for, for plants and trees, and especially because they're growing and growing over time. So individuation for him is symbolized by a tree.

00:26:14.347 --> 00:26:43.030
And unfolding of the personality in individuation is also represented in form of the lotus or of the rose based on where you are, because the rose is the lotus of the West and the lotus is the symbol of individuation on the East, which is why the next event, individuation, has as a title the little lotus coming out of the water, which is a pretty symbolic picture fitting for this.

00:26:47.510 --> 00:27:14.441
So everything that's dark and a little bit wet and murky, for example, a dark forest or a cave symbolizing the unconscious, but very often also the feminine aspect, because in yin and yang, the Yin is female and it's dark and it's associated with the wet side of the mountain.

00:27:15.282 --> 00:27:38.245
So these associations are conglomerated very often together and very often associated with male and female. And the male, for those who are familiar with tarot, is very associated with, let's say, intellect.

00:27:38.422 --> 00:28:02.210
So you have this sword cards and they are all very negative and they are associated with the mind and this separating, differentiating part of the mind because it's what we do. And this is why the hero very often has a sword. Because he can skillfully cut apart things.

00:28:02.485 --> 00:28:18.676
Siegfried, for example, in the German myth, he cuts the dragon into pieces and uses this as material. I think he builds an armor out of the scales, for example. And this is then what consciousness does.

00:28:18.773 --> 00:28:41.817
It takes this dragon of chaos, the unconscious dragon, and brings it into usable parts through the intellect, through the thought, thought. But then you, of course, you have this feminine aspect, which is very often symbolized by the cup, because the cup brings things together and holds things together.

00:28:42.989 --> 00:29:03.622
The female principle for Jung being something that's not separating like the male principle, but something that's combining and holding together and also sheltering. And protecting things. So vases, cups, but also caves being in that regard associated with the female principle.

00:29:06.495 --> 00:29:25.397
Of course, not one thing is better than the other. Both have their purpose and their things to do. The question is then how to combine both again. So really a lot about animals.

00:29:25.998 --> 00:29:45.787
So birds symbolizing thoughts, bears symbolizing the female animalistic side, lions symbolizing very often the August heat. So this very aggressive tendency.

00:29:48.011 --> 00:30:12.857
And this unification process is something that is not really happening in the, in the Western sphere. Jung talked about this a lot. So he was interested in the unification process. You have the opposites, you even have the third trying to combine both.

00:30:13.683 --> 00:30:30.480
But if you look into Western stories, very often the story ends that you kill the dragon, that you beat evil, right? So it's rather a further separation by destroying everything which is not the other thing.

00:30:32.492 --> 00:30:47.141
They said this is a rather shortcoming of the Western mind and the Western culture, that it's not integrating the things because the unification is an integration process. It's the growth of consciousness, it's the integration of foreign parts.

00:30:47.639 --> 00:31:05.600
So you can be afraid of your shadow side, but this does not mean that you should get rid of your shadow side, but rather of finding ways to integrate it into your consciousness because it's part of you missing that. Preventing reaching a higher state.

00:31:06.442 --> 00:31:26.126
So, but if you look into most Western stories, the bad guys get beaten and then they disintegrate or die or whatever. So they get depotentiated, basically. They don't have any more power. But in the East, he says, the stories go a little bit different.

00:31:27.347 --> 00:31:48.365
In the East, the dragon is a positive sign because in the West, only the negative sign is looked at, uh, in the form of the dragon. So it's, it's has sharp teeth, it can fly, it is big, it is strong, it can breathe fire. So it's only death and destruction.

00:31:49.200 --> 00:32:12.449
That's so all the Western heroes like St. Patrick or St. George, they kill snakes, they kill dragons, and that's why they're the heroes. But they are just, let's say, repress the negative aspects. But in the East, the dragon is a positive symbol because at the same time, something is not purely negative.

00:32:13.411 --> 00:32:25.132
There's also something positive if it's possible to integrate it, to unify it. And bring it together. But that's a very great and difficult trick.

00:32:26.578 --> 00:32:45.204
So one big unifier for Jung, of course, was Jesus, as he, again, like most hero stories, he has parts of both worlds. He is the son of God, but he's also God, and he's the son of man also.

00:32:45.982 --> 00:33:08.245
So he grew up like a normal human being until he found out that he is the son of God or that he is God. And he's trying to relieve the world of the sins. But that's the story of, again, having those two worlds being combined in one person. But in the end, he gets killed by human beings.

00:33:08.342 --> 00:33:31.331
And This is where Jung tried to find more and more in the whole alchemical process to really have a psychological basis where people try to investigate this shortcomings, let's say, of Western culture.

00:33:31.962 --> 00:33:46.279
Not really shortcomings, but let's say earlier stages of psychological development of, of the culture. As he sees alchemy building on top of Christianity and trying to develop those ideas further.

00:33:46.664 --> 00:34:09.408
And the alchemical process is again, a process of combining things, of unifying things, things that cannot be unified normally. So you try to create a substance which is— what was it? It is hot, cold, moist, and dry.

00:34:10.132 --> 00:34:27.542
So you have contrasting opposite characteristics that you try to combine into one essence to bring together incompatible things to reach something of a higher quality because they are all just parts and facets.

00:34:28.272 --> 00:34:47.071
But in the end, they're the facets of a singular thing, and this thing is supposed to be brought into existence through this process of separation and combining again. So this alchemical process is always, okay, I take in one part and then I try to get it back in and try to recombine it.

00:34:50.397 --> 00:35:07.856
And this is the whole psychological processes happening inside of people. As soon as you have consciousness, you are in contrast to the world. You are in contrast to the unconscious.

00:35:08.371 --> 00:35:31.244
It's already a defiance of the world as you now move away from how the world is supposed to be. So when you have animals, they have their instincts and they just follow their instincts and so on. But human beings, We have free will and we can decide what to do. So we can step out of line and do something different.

00:35:31.503 --> 00:35:44.805
But this, of course, brings into force all those other forces that try to get people back into line. And this is where the tension occurs. And this is where everything happens.

00:35:45.319 --> 00:36:05.047
And this is why you have in stories always these contrasts of A very rich man meeting a poor girl, like in Pretty Woman, where it's then the question, okay, how will they then combine and find together? How will they transform through their interactions?

00:36:05.160 --> 00:36:19.808
Because most stories are about the interactions and relations between the protagonist and someone else, and through the story being transformed in their personality and in their worldview and everything.

00:36:19.857 --> 00:36:40.583
This process happens through the change in mediation through the third and a certain symbol which is signalizing the way where people are supposed to go to have this bridge to the unconscious and bringing these parts into consciousness.

00:36:40.876 --> 00:37:01.853
So for Jung, all those cultural Artefacts, for example, in religion or in storytelling, are supposed to be that bridge into the unconscious to make these unconscious dark things graspable. To say, okay, at least then I have an approximation of something.

00:37:02.383 --> 00:37:21.484
There's not something completely overwhelming coming, but rather some little bit like training ground of preparation. How anger can feel like and how love can feel like and how it looks like and how it's supposed to interact with the rest of the world.

00:37:21.532 --> 00:37:41.595
This is why you had in the ancient Greek mythology all those emotions symbolized by persons like Ares for war and Aphrodite for love and Athena for wisdom. To have approximation. Okay, they are like this. They get characterized.

00:37:41.852 --> 00:38:00.479
They have a personality and you see how they're interacting in their stories to give people an approximation to their own psychology and how to just order things. Because ordering is already a conscious process and culture is supposed to support this.

00:38:00.927 --> 00:38:25.600
But for Jung, this connection is diminished since Nietzsche's death of God, which is due to this depotentiation of cultural symbols that don't have the same meaning anymore. They don't generate the same fascination. They become stale. They're just copied and copied and copied.

00:38:25.922 --> 00:38:43.014
So they have to be believed instead of being believed all by themselves. So suddenly faith becomes this really conscious effort, willful effort of I have to believe this, and not I'm so convinced by it that I don't question it.

00:38:44.600 --> 00:39:02.333
So, but he says humanity, and especially the West, has developed in a direction where new symbols are necessary to explain this new psychological needs and ground that is now there.

00:39:03.075 --> 00:39:27.349
So with time, these symbols will come up very likely individual in, in persons, many different persons at the same time and their dreams and their visions and their fantasies. And they will try to bring them out into the world, uh, where they will reach other persons again. And it can become a unifying symbol that then gives meaning.

00:39:27.445 --> 00:39:44.997
Of course, this can be something then like the swastika, which was really not a good thing to come out of the unconscious, which was horrible, but which can, in a, let's say, positive light, be something that is more unifying to people.

00:39:45.320 --> 00:40:12.488
And I hope I was clear and be understandable what I tried to get at with the opposites, with the third, and with the unification, which are very, very fundamental principles for Jung and how the psyche experiences the world and is changed through existence in the world.

00:40:13.241 --> 00:40:23.305
And how it is changed and can change itself. And yeah, I want to thank you for listening.

